6862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$"] ["text" "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"] ["text" "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"] ["text" "The series is divergent because $|r|<1.$"] ["text" "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-67459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series \n ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-4){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2n}$ ?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-67459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2(1)}}{(-4){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2(0)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-67459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-67459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-67459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$"] ["text" "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"] ["text" "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"] ["text" "The series is divergent because $|r|<1.$"] ["text" "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-67459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $1+5+25+125+⋯$\n- $\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯$\n- $0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $1+5+25+125+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{7})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.01}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series III converges." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-40282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $-1+1-1+1-⋯$\n- $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$\n- $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $-1+1-1+1-⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.11}{0.1} \\\\  & =1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent  because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-124912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{0.25{(1.1)}^{1}}{0.25{(1.1)}^{0}} \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$ starts with $n=2.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{3-1}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{3}^{2-1}}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})(\\frac{{2}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-124906" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124906" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124906" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$\n- $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$\n- $-8+16-32+64-⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-2}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $-8+16-32+64-⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{16}{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and II converge." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-40277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-50506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-50506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=5.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{8{(2)}^{2}}{8{(2)}^{1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\frac{{8}^{1+1}}{{16}^{1-1}}}{\\frac{{8}^{0+1}}{{16}^{0-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})}{(\\frac{{8}^{1}}{{16}^{-1}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})(\\frac{{16}^{-1}}{{8}^{1}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{1})(\\frac{1}{16\\cdot 8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series  I and III converge." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-50506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-50506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-50506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-50506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-347229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-347229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{3}^{8}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{9}^{4}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{k-4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{k-4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{k-4}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{k-4} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{k-4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{k-4}=1$ has the solution $k=4.$ Now, since ${9}^{k-4}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{k-4}<1$ must be $k<4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-347229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-347229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-347229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-347229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-347229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn-1}}{{100}^{n}}$ converge?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn-1}}{{100}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn}}{{100}^{n}\\cdot 10} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{10}^{k}}{100} & <1 \\\\ {10}^{k} & <100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${10}^{k}=100$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since ${10}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${10}^{k}<100$ must be $k<2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-124924" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124924" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k<1"] ["math" "k<2"] ["math" "k>0"] ["math" "k>1"] ["math" "k>2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-124924" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k<2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}}$ converge?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn}\\cdot 16}{{4}^{n}\\cdot {4}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(16\\cdot 4){(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }64{(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{16}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {16}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${16}^{k}=4$ has the solution $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Now, since ${16}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${16}^{k}<4$ must be $k<\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-40590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k<1"] ["math" "k<2"] ["math" "k<\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "k>0"] ["math" "k>\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-40590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k<\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-78490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}})}^{n}$ converge?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-78490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{64}{{2}^{k}} & <1 \\\\ 64 & <{2}^{k} \\\\ {2}^{k} & >64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{64}{{2}^{k}}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${2}^{k}=64$ has the solution $k=6.$ Now, since ${2}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${2}^{k}>64$ must be $k>6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-78490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-78490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-78490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k<0"] ["math" "k<6"] ["math" "k>0"] ["math" "k>5"] ["math" "k>6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-78490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k>6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-351372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-351372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{3}^{6}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{3-k})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{3-k}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{3-k}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{3-k} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{3-k}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{3-k}=1$ has the solution $k=3.$ Now, since ${9}^{3-k}$ decreases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{3-k}<1$ must be $k>3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-351372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "104f8df4135418ef1e9a94af567be0b74b08e060298dd02c72ccab26c7f9795e"} {"question" "q-351372" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-351372" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math ">"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-351372" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "q-351372" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf9702615da1f1f534f3d017bb221dffc80550eadaa1186862ccfc0e42c0e796"} {"question" "e-689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{n+1}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/example-689.json" "file_sha256" "a7faf0dd8040f231154b55559a78c29ab31012d31b19440c1d1313e545cc86b0"} {"question" "e-689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2+1}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| <1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/example-689.json" "file_sha256" "a7faf0dd8040f231154b55559a78c29ab31012d31b19440c1d1313e545cc86b0"} {"question" "e-691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty {3}\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^{n}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/example-691.json" "file_sha256" "d04aba4cff543cc22605e77ebea56d7feba31df1ef6a6bd59cf4eddbd2c9b012"} {"question" "e-691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}}{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/example-691.json" "file_sha256" "d04aba4cff543cc22605e77ebea56d7feba31df1ef6a6bd59cf4eddbd2c9b012"} {"question" "e-693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/example-693.json" "file_sha256" "6ad38c126482de3c7dd030c0e266cfb8ddea6ffcc11c5a1e74f79eecf7bf4992"} {"question" "e-693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(7)}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{28}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{24}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/example-693.json" "file_sha256" "6ad38c126482de3c7dd030c0e266cfb8ddea6ffcc11c5a1e74f79eecf7bf4992"} {"question" "e-6773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which values of $k$ does the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty\\dfrac{3(2)^{kn}}{4^n}$ converge?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/example-6773.json" "file_sha256" "f8f58330fcd7b915dd581053b9ea56f31f057c38be20f404efd12e9bed0cab73"} {"question" "e-6773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a r^n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(2)^{kn}}{{4}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3\\frac{{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r = \\dfrac{2^k}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r| < 1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {2}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\dfrac{2^k}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032261/example-6773.json" "file_sha256" "f8f58330fcd7b915dd581053b9ea56f31f057c38be20f404efd12e9bed0cab73"}] "usage" {"e-689" [] "e-691" [] "e-6773" [] "e-693" []} "basis" 1220 "retractions" [[17592186078266 :question/problem "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n$S = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{n+1}$"] [17592186078266 :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1$. So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} &= (\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})^{2 + 1})/(\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})^{1 + 1}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})^{3})/(\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $\\mid r \\mid <1$."] [17592186078268 :question/problem "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n$\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty {3}\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^{n}$"] [17592186078268 :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=0$. So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\frac{a_{1}}{a_{0}} &= (3(\\frac{3}{2})^{1})/(3(\\frac{3}{2})^{0}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because\n\n$$\n\\mid r \\mid \\geq 1\n$$"] [17592186078269 :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$\n2. $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$\n3. $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$"] [17592186078269 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$ starts with $n=1$. So the common ratio is\n$r = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}}|= (4(-3)^{2})/(4(-3)^{1}); =-3$.\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $\\mid r \\mid \\geq 1$.\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$ starts with $n=6$. So the common ratio is\n$r = \\frac{a_{7}}{a_{6}}|= (32(\\frac{1}{2})^{4(7)})/(32(\\frac{1}{2})^{4(6)}); = (32(\\frac{1}{2})^{28})/(32(\\frac{1}{2})^{24}); = (\\frac{1}{2})^{4}; = \\frac{1}{16}$.\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $\\mid r \\mid < 1$.\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$ starts with $n=0$. So the common ratio is\n$r = \\frac{a_{1}}{a_{0}}|= ((\\frac{4^{1}}{2^{1 + 3}}))/((\\frac{4^{0}}{2^{0 + 3}})); = ((\\frac{4^{1}}{2^{4}}))/((\\frac{4^{0}}{2^{3}})); = (\\frac{4^{1}}{2^{4}})(\\frac{2^{3}}{4^{0}}); = \\frac{4}{2}; = 2$.\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $\\mid r \\mid \\geq 1$.\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges."] [17592186078264 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a r^n\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(3(2)^{kn})/(4^{n}) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(3(2^{k})^{n})/(4^{n}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)3((2^{k})^{n})/(4^{n}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)3(\\frac{2^{k}}{4})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{2^k}{4}\n$$\n\nFor the series to converge, we require $\\mid r \\mid < 1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid \\frac{2^{k}}{4} \\mid \\mid < 1; \\frac{2^{k}}{4}\\begin{vmatrix}< 1 \\\\ 2^{k}\\end{vmatrix}< 4\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2^k}{4}\n$$\n\nis always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2$. Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2$."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
